NNAAMMEE
     llddaappdd — Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llddaappdd [--ddnnvv] [--DD _m_a_c_r_o=_v_a_l_u_e] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--rr _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y] [--ss _f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     llddaappdd is a daemon which implements version 3 of the LDAP protocol.

     A running llddaappdd process can be controlled using the ldapctl(8) utility.

     The options are as follows:

     --DD _m_a_c_r_o=_v_a_l_u_e
             Define _m_a_c_r_o to be set to _v_a_l_u_e on the command line.  Overrides
             the definition of _m_a_c_r_o in the configuration file.

     --dd      Do not daemonize and log to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Use _f_i_l_e as the configuration file, instead of the default
             _/_e_t_c_/_l_d_a_p_d_._c_o_n_f.

     --nn      Configtest mode.  Only check the configuration file for validity.

     --rr _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
             Store and read database files in _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y, instead of the de‐
             fault _/_v_a_r_/_d_b_/_l_d_a_p.

     --ss _f_i_l_e
             Specify an alternative location for the socket file.

     --vv      Produce more verbose output.  A second --vv together with the --dd
             flag produces debug traces of decoded BER messages on stderr.

AAUUTTHHEENNTTIICCAATTIIOONN
     llddaappdd can authenticate users via simple binds or SASL with the PLAIN
     mechanism.

     When using simple binds, the bind DN entry must exist in a namespace and
     have a uusseerrPPaasssswwoorrdd attribute.  The following formats of the uusseerrPPaasssswwoorrdd
     attribute are recognized:

     {{SSHHAA}}ddiiggeesstt
             Verify the password against the SHA-1 digest.

     {{SSSSHHAA}}ddiiggeesstt
             Verify the password against the salted SHA-1 digest.

     {{CCRRYYPPTT}}hhaasshh
             Verify the password against the crypt(3) hash.

     {{BBSSDDAAUUTTHH}}uusseerrnnaammee
             Use BSD Authentication with the given username and authentication
             style “auth-ldap”.  This is similar to using SASL PLAIN authenti‐
             cation with _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e as the authentication ID.

     {{BBSSDDAAUUTTHH}}uusseerrnnaammee##ccllaassss
             Same as above, but overrides the login class.

     Without a prefix, the uusseerrPPaasssswwoorrdd attribute is compared literally with
     the provided plain text password.

     When using SASL binds, the authentication ID should be a valid username
     for BSD Authentication.

     For plain text passwords to be accepted, the connection must be consid‐
     ered secure, either by using an encrypted connection, or by using the
     sseeccuurree keyword in the configuration file.

